## Why This Is Asked

A practical exercise to gauge understanding of rate limiting, multi-tenant isolation, and backpressure in an LLM gateway.

## Key Concepts

- Token-bucket rate limiting
- Per-tenant quotas
- Backpressure and fairness
- Observability and tracing

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo: simple token bucket for a tenant
class Bucket {
  constructor(capacity, refillPerMs) {
    this.capacity = capacity;
    this.tokens = capacity;
    this.refillPerMs = refillPerMs;
    this.last = Date.now();
  }
  take(n = 1) {
    const now = Date.now();
    const elapsed = now - this.last;
    this.tokens = Math.min(this.capacity, this.tokens + elapsed * this.refillPerMs);
    this.last = now;
    if (this.tokens >= n) {
      this.tokens -= n;
      return true;
    }
    return false;
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would bucket state persist across restarts?
- How would you scale this across many gateway instances?

## Why This Is Asked
Reasoning about per-tenant budgeting mitigates abuse and cost across tenants.

## Key Concepts
- Per-tenant budgets, atomic accounting, and race-free updates
- Simple routing based on budget state and policy
- Basic test plan with concurrency checks

## Code Example
```javascript
// pseudo: atomic deduct using Redis Lua script
async function tryDeduct(redis, key, cost){
  const script = `return redis.call('DECRBY', KEYS[1], ARGV[1])`;
  const remaining = await redis.eval(script, 1, key, cost);
  return remaining >= 0;
}
```

